A contained lab can reproduce the behavior with two pages on loopback and a destination that is visibly marked as a training page. In a disposable Express project, the server-side case can use a conditional route such as:

```js
app.get('/', (req, res, next) => {
  const labCase = req.get('X-Lab-Case') === 'redirect';
  const mobile = /Android|iPhone/i.test(req.get('user-agent') || '');
  if (labCase && mobile) return res.redirect(302, '/local-training-landing');
  next();
});
```

The special header ensures the rule cannot trigger during an ordinary visit, and the destination remains `/local-training-landing` on `127.0.0.1`. The landing page should say `LOCAL TEST ONLY`, contain no form fields, and load no remote assets. Run the server bound to loopback, use synthetic requests, and delete the project after the exercise. A browser-side case can place `window.location.assign('/local-training-landing')` behind a labeled test button in a separate page. Comparing the two cases teaches whether the navigation came from an HTTP status and `Location` header or from client-side code.

For production investigation, capture the initiating URL, response status, headers, script stack, and service-worker state before changing files. Compare route handlers, shared templates, generated bundles, CMS themes, reverse-proxy rewrites, and deployment manifests with a verified release. Review administrator creation, password resets, session tokens, API-key use, plugin updates, and source-control history. Inspect service-worker registrations and caches because a browser can preserve a navigation after the server is clean. Check whether the redirect is conditional on user agent, referrer, cookie, path, or time, and test from more than one browser and network.

Preserve access and authentication logs, file hashes, audit events, container image digests, browser captures, and a copy of suspicious responses. Isolate a compromised host, revoke sessions, rotate secrets, remove unauthorized accounts, restore signed code, invalidate caches, and verify all routes. Prevention includes multi-factor authentication, protected branches, least privilege, dependency review, content-security policy, integrity monitoring, and synthetic checks. The conditional code above is useful only inside the loopback lab; it must never be adapted to an external destination or exposed to visitors.

Use a disposable application bound to `127.0.0.1` and two neutral pages, `/home` and `/local-training-landing`. The landing page should display `LOCAL REDIRECT EXERCISE`, contain no fields, and load no remote assets. For a server-side case, make the trigger explicit and local:

```js
app.get('/home', (req, res, next) => {
  const exercise = req.get('X-Exercise') === 'server';
  const localReferrer = (req.get('referer') || '').includes('127.0.0.1');
  if (exercise && localReferrer) return res.redirect(302, '/local-training-landing');
  next();
});
```

